Let me change my example a little bit:

Bkl A: 1 to 10 (common language items) 11 to 20 (language unique to form A)  21 to 30 (math unique to form A)
Bkl B  1 to 10 (common language items) , 31 to 40 (language unique to form B) and 41-50 (math unique to form B)

Now we have 10 items in common (1-10) but only \ language .
True model is a bi-factor with a general factor and two group factors (language and math).

My understanding is that factor analysis may infer general factor loadings / language group factor but will not identify
correctly one math group factor. Instead it will break math group factors into two group factors since it doesn't have information on how items 21 to 30 is associated to items 41-50.

Is that correct ?

Best
Ricardo

